          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Apart from medication, what helps with long-term insomnia?",
          "answer": "Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, ahead of medication. The guideline summary notes that its effects tend to last after treatment ends, and that it avoids the risks of long-term sleep medicine. It is usually delivered over about four to eight sessions. Two of its components are described: **stimulus control** (use the bed only for sleep, and get up when you cannot sleep) and **sleep restriction** (limit time in bed toward your actual sleep time, then extend it gradually). *(Source: guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line)*\n\nDaily habits also matter. Caffeine has a long half-life, so an afternoon dose can still be active at bedtime. A common approach is an early-afternoon cut-off. Alcohol shortens the time to fall asleep but fragments the second half of the night, and nicotine withdrawal during the night can contribute to early waking. Short early-afternoon naps are compatible with good sleep for most people, but long or late naps can weaken the sleep pressure that helps you fall asleep. *(Source: lifestyle-insomnia-sleep)*\n\nSome signs suggest that a doctor should look into the cause, not just adjust habits. These include loud snoring with pauses in breathing, excessive daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, acting out dreams, and sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness. *(Source: condition-insomnia)*\n\nThe retrieved material does not give the full CBT-I programme or its evidence in detail, and it does not say which therapists or services are available to you. A doctor or sleep specialist can explain these and check whether something else is contributing.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
